+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Preserve Concurrent Edits as Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers can independently correct the same vegetation survey visit while tablets are disconnected. A visit contains fields whose scientific meaning can cross structural boundaries: a species identification can qualify its cover estimate, notes can explain an otherwise surprising value, and observer changes can affect how a coordinator interprets the record. The record store must retain every accepted edit, identify a usable current state, and expose unresolved disagreement before reviewed CSV export.
+
+The concurrency policy determines more than server persistence. It determines what state tablets retain and submit, what coordinators compare, how provenance is represented, and which visits are eligible for export.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **No accepted edit may be silently discarded.** Every accepted submission must remain independently retrievable with its author and receipt time. This gate follows the HLD tenet to avoid silently discarding submitted edits.
+- **Unresolved disagreement must be visible to export.** A concurrency outcome must identify when a visit requires review so review-export can keep it out of the partner CSV.
+- **Offline submission must remain possible.** The policy cannot require a tablet to coordinate with the server or another tablet while a worker edits a local copy.
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Preservation of scientific edit context: major.** The policy should avoid separating or recombining values in ways that alter the meaning of a worker's submitted observation. This turns on the HLD tenet to keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.
+- **Review workload: major.** Two coordinators must be able to clear the queue before the weekly export; unnecessary conflicts consume limited review time.
+- **Explainable provenance: major.** A coordinator and future auditor should be able to reconstruct which complete submissions contributed to the current visit.
+- **Merge-policy maintenance: moderate.** Rules that combine records must remain correct as fields and scientific practices evolve, without requiring a dedicated systems administrator.
+- **Offline client state and submission cost: moderate.** Tablets operate offline for up to 48 hours and should retain enough state to submit safely without an unnecessarily complex synchronization model.
+- **Future reversibility: minor.** The first-release policy should not destroy evidence needed to adopt more selective automation after operational experience establishes where it is safe.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each edit is a complete immutable revision naming the server revision from which it began. An uncontested edit advances the current revision. An edit based on another starting point becomes a competing revision, and all subsequent edits remain reviewable until a coordinator creates a resolved whole-visit revision.
+
+Tablet-sync retains a base revision ID with each editable copy, submits a complete visit and stable idempotency key, and handles an acceptance result that either advances the visit or requires review. It does not calculate a structured field delta or reproduce server merge rules.
+
+Review-export compares complete revisions, exposes their submission provenance, and excludes every visit with an open review case. A resolution records all revisions reviewed and creates a new complete current revision, even when the coordinator selects one contender unchanged.
+
+- **No accepted edit may be silently discarded: passes.** Every submission and its complete payload remain immutable and retrievable.
+- **Unresolved disagreement must be visible to export: passes.** A review case explicitly separates unresolved visits from export-eligible visits.
+- **Offline submission must remain possible: passes.** A tablet needs its local visit and one base revision ID, not an online lock or current server state.
+- **Preservation of scientific edit context: strong fit.** Values submitted together remain together until a coordinator deliberately produces a resolution.
+- **Review workload: weak fit.** Two edits to unrelated fields still compete, and repeated offline corrections can place several complete revisions in one case.
+- **Explainable provenance: strong fit.** The store can trace a resolution to complete reviewed revisions without explaining automatic field-level composition.
+- **Merge-policy maintenance: strong fit.** The server needs conflict detection and resolution state transitions but no field-specific combination rules.
+- **Offline client state and submission cost: partial fit.** Client state is simple, but every edit transmits and stores a complete visit payload.
+- **Future reversibility: strong fit.** Complete original submissions preserve the evidence needed to identify recurring safe merge patterns later.
+
+The essential tradeoff is a larger review queue in exchange for preserving each worker's observation as a coherent unit.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each edit carries its base snapshot and structured changes. The server compares the base, current state, and incoming changes. Changes to distinct fields or collection entries combine automatically; overlapping changes become review work. Lists such as observers and keyed species entries require explicit identity, insertion, deletion, and ordering rules. Provenance follows each resulting field or collection entry rather than only the complete visit.
+
+Tablet-sync retains base snapshots, computes or records structured changes, submits them with stable identifiers, and understands enough of the merge contract to explain whether changes were combined or held for review. Changes to the visit schema or merge semantics can require local-state migration and coordinated client/server rollout.
+
+Review-export presents field-level provenance and overlapping changes. It must distinguish automatically combined values from manually resolved values and decide whether an automatically merged visit is immediately exportable or requires coordinator approval.
+
+- **No accepted edit may be silently discarded: passes.** Original submissions can remain immutable even when the server also creates a combined representation.
+- **Unresolved disagreement must be visible to export: passes.** Overlapping changes can create explicit review work.
+- **Offline submission must remain possible: passes.** A tablet can retain its base snapshot and construct changes without connectivity.
+- **Preservation of scientific edit context: weak fit.** Structural independence does not guarantee scientific independence; rules can combine related edits without recognizing their relationship.
+- **Review workload: strong fit.** Correctly classified disjoint edits bypass manual review, potentially reducing the queue substantially.
+- **Explainable provenance: partial fit.** Field-level lineage is precise but more complex, particularly for collection edits and values produced by repeated merges.
+- **Merge-policy maintenance: weak fit.** Scalar fields, observer lists, species entries, notes, and future schema additions need explicit and maintained semantics.
+- **Offline client state and submission cost: weak fit.** Base snapshots, structured changes, schema evolution, and merge outcomes create a larger synchronization contract, although deltas may reduce transfer size.
+- **Future reversibility: partial fit.** Retained submissions preserve source evidence, but clients and review tooling become coupled to field-level merge semantics.
+
+This approach remains credible because review capacity is limited and many concurrent edits may prove genuinely independent. It becomes more attractive if operational data shows that whole-visit conflicts routinely threaten the Friday export, and if the team can define scientifically safe merge units rather than assuming field separation implies independence.
+
+## Selection
+
+The record store preserves concurrent edits as whole-visit revisions and sends competing revisions to coordinator review.
+
+Both options satisfy the gates, and field-aware merging is stronger on review workload. Whole-visit revisions win because preservation of scientific edit context and explainable provenance carry equal or greater importance: the store must not silently manufacture a scientifically misleading combination merely to reduce queue size. This selection applies the HLD priorities to keep submitted observations inspectable and to favor a workflow maintainable by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator. The team accepts a larger review queue rather than maintaining merge rules that cannot reliably recognize relationships among scientific fields.
+
+This choice forecloses automatic combination in the first release, not permanently. Complete submissions and revision provenance are retained so field-aware automation can be reconsidered if measured queue volume is unsustainable and domain-specific safe merge boundaries can be stated and tested.
+
+The selection creates these downstream obligations:
+
+- Record-store must keep complete submissions and revisions immutable, detect stale bases, freeze the comparison anchor while review is open, and create an auditable resolution revision.
+- Tablet-sync must retain a base revision ID for each editable server-backed visit, send complete payloads with retry-stable idempotency keys, and represent both current and review-required acceptance outcomes. Its local workflow must not assume acceptance means the submitted revision became current.
+- Review-export must provide complete-revision comparison and provenance, resolve against the exact set of revisions reviewed, and withhold every visit with an open review case from CSV export.
+
+The tablet-sync and review-export nodes own the implementation and user-experience details of those obligations; they do not redefine record-store concurrency semantics.
