-## Downstream Contract Work
+## Revision Advancement and Conflict Detection
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The first accepted submission for a visit becomes current when no current revision exists. After that:
+
+- When no review case is open and a submission names the current revision as its base, its new revision becomes current.
+- When no review case is open and a submission names any other base, or names no base, the store opens a review case containing the current revision and the new competing revision. The current pointer does not move.
+- While a review case is open, every accepted worker submission joins that case and receives `review_required`, including one based on the current revision. The current pointer remains fixed so the comparison anchor cannot move during review.
+
+The store performs no field-level merge and does not infer equivalence from payload values. Two independently submitted identical payloads remain distinct revisions if idempotency does not identify them as one request.
+
+## Retrieval and History
+
+Current-state retrieval returns the visit ID, plot ID, complete current revision, and an explicit review status. When review is open, the response also identifies the review case and all competing revision IDs, including the current revision used as the comparison anchor. A caller can retrieve each complete revision and its provenance.
+
+History retrieval returns accepted submissions, generated revisions, review-case membership, and resolution records in server receipt order. Receipt order is audit information only; it does not establish scientific precedence. Rejected requests are not revisions, though operational logging may retain them outside visit history.
+
+## Review Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves the currently open review case by submitting a complete whole-visit payload, the review case ID, the full set of revision IDs reviewed, and a new idempotency key. The resolution may reproduce one contender exactly or reconcile information manually, but it always creates a new revision so the coordinator's act and identity remain explicit.
+
+The store accepts a resolution only when its review case is still open and its reviewed revision set exactly matches the case's current membership. A stale resolution is rejected without changing the case. An accepted resolution records the coordinator identity, resolution time, reviewed revision IDs, and resulting revision ID; it closes the case and atomically makes the resulting revision current.
+
+A retry of an accepted resolution follows the same idempotency rules as a worker submission. Reuse of its key with changed content is rejected.
+
+## Consistency and Atomicity
+
+Acceptance of a submission, creation of its revision, assignment of its disposition, and any review-case update occur atomically. Resolution creation, review-case closure, and current-pointer advancement also occur atomically. Concurrent transactions are serialized per visit so two edits that both observe the same current revision cannot both advance it: one may become current and the other must open or join review.
+
+The visit's plot UUID cannot change between revisions. A request carrying a different plot UUID for an existing visit is rejected rather than treated as an edit; correcting a visit attached to the wrong plot requires a separately designed administrative operation.
+
+## Downstream Contracts
+
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID associated with an editable local copy, reuse idempotency keys across retries, submit complete visit payloads, and handle both dispositions. Its local persistence and worker-facing behavior remain for the tablet-sync design.
+
+Review-export may export only visits with a current revision and no open review case. It must compare complete competing revisions and submit resolutions against exact review-case membership. Its comparison interface, queue ordering, and CSV production remain for the review-export design.
+
+These obligations cross segment boundaries and require confirmation before their sibling designs are created or changed.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit unit | Preserve complete competing visit revisions for coordinator resolution ([decision](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge of unrelated edits | Whole revisions preserve each worker's scientific context and avoid merge rules that can combine related observations without recognizing their relationship; the resulting larger review queue is acceptable. |
+| Current state during review | Freeze the last uncontested current revision until resolution | Advance a branch while retaining competitors; remove the current designation entirely | A stable comparison anchor keeps retrieval deterministic and prevents arrival order from selecting among competitors, while retaining a usable state for continued field work. |
+| Resolution representation | Create a new complete revision for every coordinator resolution | Point current directly at a selected contender when unchanged | A distinct revision records the coordinator's decision uniformly whether they select or reconcile a contender. |
+| Resolution concurrency | Require exact review-case membership | Resolve a snapshot and leave later contenders for another case | Exact membership prevents a coordinator from unknowingly closing review while an accepted contender remains unresolved. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Define the administrative operation for correcting a visit associated with the wrong plot; ordinary revision submission cannot change visit identity.
+2. Set retention and access-control policies for operational logs of rejected requests outside immutable visit history.
